            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "xxviii   Contents"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "index",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.076,
            0.1,
            0.475,
            0.741
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Coupling Nucleotide Excision Repair to Transcription Ensures That the Cell's Most Important DNA Is Efficiently Repaired 290  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Chemistry of the DNA Bases Facilitates Damage Detection 290  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Special Translesion DNA Polymerases Are Used in Emergencies 292  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Double-Strand Breaks Are Efficiently Repaired 292  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "DNA Damage Delays Progression of the Cell Cycle 295  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 295  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "HOMOLOGOUS RECOMBINATION 296  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Homologous Recombination Has Common Features in All Cells 296  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "DNA Base-pairing Guides Homologous Recombination 296  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Homologous Recombination Can Flawlessly Repair Double-Strand Breaks in DNA 297  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Specialized Processing of Double-Strand Breaks Commits Repair to Homologous Recombination 298  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Strand Exchange Is Directed by the RecA/Rad51 Protein 298  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Homologous Recombination Can Rescue Broken and Stalled DNA Replication Forks 299  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "DNA Repair by Homologous Recombination Entails Risks to the Cell 300  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Homologous Recombination Is Crucial for Meiosis 301  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Meiotic Recombination Begins with a Programmed Double-Strand Break 302  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Holliday Junctions Are Recognized by Enzymes That Drive Branch Migration 302  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Homologous Recombination Produces Crossovers Between Maternal and Paternal Chromosomes During Meiosis 304  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Homologous Recombination Often Results in Gene Conversion 305  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 306  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "TRANSPOSITION AND CONSERVATIVE SITE-SPECIFIC RECOMBINATION 306  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Through Transposition, Mobile Genetic Elements Can Insert into Any DNA Sequence 307  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "DNA-only Transposons Can Move by a Cut-and-Paste Mechanism 307  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Some DNA-only Transposons Move by Replicating Themselves 309  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Some Viruses Use a Transposition Mechanism to Move Themselves into Host-Cell Chromosomes 309  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Some RNA Viruses Replicate and Express Their Genomes Without Using DNA as an Intermediate 311  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Retroviral-like Retrotransposons Resemble Retroviruses, but Cannot Move from Cell to Cell 313  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Large Fraction of the Human Genome Is Composed of Nonretroviral Retrotransposons 313  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Different Transposable Elements Predominate in Different Organisms 314  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Genome Sequences Reveal the Approximate Times at Which Transposable Elements Have Moved 314  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Conservative Site-specific Recombination Can Reversibly Rearrange DNA 315  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Conservative Site-specific Recombination Can Be Used to Turn Genes On or Off 316  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Bacterial Conservative Site-specific Recombinases Have Become Powerful Tools for Cell and Developmental Biologists 317  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 317  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Problems 318  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "References 320"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.078,
            0.754,
            0.471,
            0.784
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter 6 How Cells Read the Genome: From DNA to Protein 321"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "index",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.077,
            0.786,
            0.474,
            0.935
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "FROM DNA TO RNA 323  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "RNA Molecules Are Single-Stranded 324  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Transcription Produces RNA Complementary to One Strand of DNA 325  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "RNA Polymerases Carry Out DNA Transcription 325  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cells Produce Different Categories of RNA Molecules 327  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Signals Encoded in DNA Tell RNA Polymerase Where to Start and Stop 328  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Bacterial Transcription Start and Stop Signals Are Heterogeneous in Nucleotide Sequence 329  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Transcription Initiation in Eukaryotes Requires Many Proteins 331  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "To Initiate Transcription, RNA Polymerase II Requires a Set of General Transcription Factors 332"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "index",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.492,
            0.1,
            0.891,
            0.937
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "In Eukaryotes, Transcription Initiation Also Requires Activator, Mediator, and Chromatin-modifying Proteins 334"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Transcription Elongation in Eukaryotes Requires Accessory Proteins 335"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Transcription Creates Superhelical Tension 335"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Transcription Elongation in Eukaryotes Is Tightly Coupled to RNA Processing 337"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "RNA Capping Is the First Modification of Eukaryotic Pre-mRNAs 338"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "RNA Splicing Removes Intron Sequences from Newly Transcribed Pre-mRNAs 339"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Nucleotide Sequences Signal Where Splicing Occurs 341"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "RNA Splicing Is Performed by the Spliceosome 341"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Spliceosome Uses ATP Hydrolysis to Produce a Complex Series of RNA-RNA Rearrangements 343"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Other Properties of Pre-mRNA and Its Synthesis Help to Explain the Choice of Proper Splice Sites 345"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "RNA Splicing Has Remarkable Plasticity 346"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Spliceosome-catalyzed RNA Splicing Evolved from RNA Self-splicing Mechanisms 347"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "RNA-processing Enzymes Generate the 3' End of Eukaryotic mRNAs 348"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Mature Eukaryotic mRNAs Are Selectively Exported from the Nucleus 349"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Noncoding RNAs Are Also Synthesized and Processed in the Nucleus 351"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Nucleolus Is a Ribosome-producing Factory 353"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Nucleus Contains a Variety of Subnuclear Biomolecular Condensates 355"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 357"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "FROM RNA TO PROTEIN 358"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "An mRNA Sequence Is Decoded in Sets of Three Nucleotides 358"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "tRNA Molecules Match Amino Acids to Codons in mRNA 359"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "tRNAs Are Covalently Modified Before They Exit from the Nucleus 361"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Specific Enzymes Couple Each Amino Acid to Its Appropriate tRNA Molecule 361"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Editing by tRNA Synthetases Ensures Accuracy 363"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Amino Acids Are Added to the C-terminal End of a Growing Polypeptide Chain 364"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The RNA Message Is Decoded in Ribosomes 365"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Elongation Factors Drive Translation Forward and Improve Its Accuracy 368"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Induced Fit and Kinetic Proofreading Help Biological Processes Overcome the Inherent Limitations of Complementary Base-Pairing 369"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Accuracy in Translation Requires a Large Expenditure of Free Energy 370"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Ribosome Is a Ribozyme 371"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Nucleotide Sequences in mRNA Signal Where to Start Protein Synthesis 373"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Stop Codons Mark the End of Translation 374"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Proteins Are Made on Polyribosomes 375"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "There Are Minor Variations in the Standard Genetic Code 375"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Inhibitors of Prokaryotic Protein Synthesis Are Useful as Antibiotics 376"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Quality-Control Mechanisms Act to Prevent Translation of Damaged mRNAs 378"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Stalled Ribosomes Can Be Rescued 379"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Ribosome Coordinates the Folding, Enzymatic Modification, and Assembly of Newly Synthesized Proteins 380"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Molecular Chaperones Help Guide the Folding of Most Proteins 380"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Proper Folding of Newly Synthesized Proteins Is Also Aided by Translation Speed and Subunit Assembly 383"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Proteins That Ultimately Fail to Fold Correctly Are Marked for Destruction by Polyubiquitin 384"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Proteasome Is a Compartmentalized Protease with Sequestered Active Sites 384"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Many Proteins Are Controlled by Regulated Destruction 386"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "There Are Many Steps from DNA to Protein 387"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 388"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "THE RNA WORLD AND THE ORIGINS OF LIFE 389"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Single-Strand RNA Molecules Can Fold into Highly Elaborate Structures 390"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Ribozymes Can Be Produced in the Laboratory 390"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
